This product includes software developed for the open implementation of the
ideal-mixing-of-complex-components (IMCC) model for silicate melt speciation.

Datapacks, benchmark sets and validation decks distributed with this software
are licensed separately under CC-BY-4.0; see LICENSE-DATA.

The thermodynamic coefficients in those datapacks derive from published work by
their respective authors, recorded per row with a provenance class. Principal
sources:

  Fegley B., Cameron A.G.W. (1987) Earth Planet. Sci. Lett. 82, 207-222.
    doi:10.1016/0012-821X(87)90196-8
  Schaefer L., Fegley B. (2004) Icarus 169, 216-241.
    doi:10.1016/j.icarus.2003.11.023
  Chase, M. W. Jr., NIST-JANAF Thermochemical Tables, 4th Edition,
    J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data Monograph 9 (1998), NIST SRD 13.
    doi:10.18434/T42S31. NIST states that these data are public and may be
    distributed or copied.
  Lamoreaux, R. H.; Hildenbrand, D. L. (1984), High Temperature Vaporization
    Behavior of Oxides. I. Alkali Metal Binary Oxides, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data
    13, 151-173. doi:10.1063/1.555706
  Lamoreaux, R. H.; Hildenbrand, D. L.; Brewer, L. (1987), High Temperature
    Vaporization Behavior of Oxides. II, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 16, 419-443.
    doi:10.1063/1.555799

The K2O(l) row is explicitly marked in PROVENANCE.yaml as a secondary,
unverified transcription from the VapoRock condensate CSV pending comparison
with the LH84 primary coefficient table.

The NIST-JANAF source records used to fit the packaged gas rows are retained
under data-src/janaf/ in source distributions and are not runtime package data.

Users of a datapack should cite the underlying source in addition to this
software.
